From 22891517d9f142721feda5e48837dad5f3d9d6b6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Ville=20Kivela=CC=88?= <vkivela@hotmail.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 15:18:27 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 13/13] extensions: answer every function the schemas declare

A function a schema declares and nothing registers is not inert. The
dispatcher reports it as an unknown API and it throws where the
extension called it. The series had marked twenty-one functions
nocompile "so the namespace and its events exist", and the namespaces
did exist, with every function in them throwing: 1Password's first pass
alone took 225 refusals across contextMenus, action, commands, privacy
and notifications.getPermissionLevel, which this series had called
deliberately inert.

So they are answered. The action's title, icon, popup and badge, and the
items an extension wants in a context menu, are kept per extension and
per tab in a registry and read back truthfully; a browser that wants to
draw any of it has it in one place. commands.getAll reports what the
manifest declares, with no shortcut, because the application has bound
none. openPopup is refused with a message the extension can read, which
is what Chrome does when there is no window to open it in.

Their schemas generate no C++ types, action.json being uncompiled in
Chrome as well, so the functions read the values they arrive as and are
registered by hand rather than through the generated registry.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
